    "topic": "ALB subnet for a new Availability Zone",
    "stem": "<p>An ecommerce website runs on Amazon EC2 instances in an Auto Scaling group behind an Application Load Balancer (ALB). A CloudOps engineer is extending the Auto Scaling group to span one more Availability Zone. EC2 instances in the new Availability Zone are in the running state but fail to register with the target group.</p>\n<p>How can the CloudOps engineer resolve this issue?</p>",
    "options": [
     {
      "key": "A",
      "html": "Attach the Availability Zone to the target group."
     },
     {
      "key": "B",
      "html": "Update inbound rules on the security groups of the EC2 instances."
     },
     {
      "key": "C",
      "html": "Attach at least one subnet in the new Availability Zone to the ALB."
     },
     {
      "key": "D",
      "html": "Update outbound rules on the security groups of the ALB."
     }
    ],
    "answer": [
     "C"
    ],
    "why": "<p>An ALB only routes to targets in Availability Zones that are enabled on the load balancer, and a zone is enabled by attaching one of its subnets to the ALB. Instances in a zone that is not enabled cannot be used as targets even though they are running.</p>",
    "whyNot": "<ul>\n<li><strong>A:</strong> Target groups have no Availability Zone attachment; zones are enabled on the load balancer.</li>\n<li><strong>B:</strong> A bad inbound rule would show targets as unhealthy, not block registration, and the existing zones work with the same security group.</li>\n<li><strong>D:</strong> ALB outbound rules would cause health check failures in all zones, not only the new one.</li>\n</ul>"
