    "n": 5,
    "topic": "<code>iam:PermissionsBoundary</code> condition key",
    "stem": "<p>A company wants to enhance its security by applying IAM permissions boundaries. The company tries to use IAM policy conditions and receives the following error:</p>\n<p>\"Error: error updating IAM Role (role-name) assume role policy: AccessDenied: User: id-arn is not authorized to perform: iam:UpdateAssumeRolePolicy on resource: role role-name status code: 403, request id: request-id\"</p>\n<p>The policy that contains the condition includes the following snippet:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-json\">{\n    &quot;Version&quot;: &quot;2012-10-17&quot;,\n    &quot;Statement&quot;: [\n        {\n            &quot;Sid&quot;: &quot;CreateOrChangeOnlyWithBoundary&quot;,\n            &quot;Effect&quot;: &quot;Allow&quot;,\n            &quot;Action&quot;: &quot;UpdateAssumeRolePolicy&quot;,\n            &quot;Resource&quot;: &quot;arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/role-name&quot;,\n            &quot;Condition&quot;: {\n                &quot;StringEquals&quot;: {\n                    &quot;iam:PermissionsBoundary&quot;: &quot;arn:aws:iam::123456789012:policy/MyBoundaryPolicy&quot;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    ]\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>What is the reason for the error?</p>",
    "options": [
     {
      "key": "A",
      "html": "Permissions boundaries are not allowed with IAM policies."
     },
     {
      "key": "B",
      "html": "IAM user id-arn is not allowed to assume role-name."
     },
     {
      "key": "C",
      "html": "Condition keys are not allowed in conjunction with permissions boundaries."
     },
     {
      "key": "D",
      "html": "The UpdateAssumeRolePolicy action is not compatible with the iam:PermissionsBoundary condition key."
     }
    ],
    "answer": [
     "D"
    ],
    "why": "<p>The <code>iam:PermissionsBoundary</code> condition key is only evaluated for a specific set of IAM actions: <code>CreateUser</code>, <code>CreateRole</code>, the put/delete permissions boundary actions, and the attach/detach/put/delete policy actions for users and roles. <code>iam:UpdateAssumeRolePolicy</code> is not one of them, so the key is absent from the request context, the <code>StringEquals</code> condition never matches, and the Allow does not apply. The result is an implicit deny and the 403.</p>",
    "whyNot": "<ul>\n<li><strong>A:</strong> Permissions boundaries are themselves IAM managed policies and work alongside identity policies.</li>\n<li><strong>B:</strong> The error is about <code>iam:UpdateAssumeRolePolicy</code> (editing the role's trust policy), not <code>sts:AssumeRole</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>C:</strong> Condition keys are valid with permissions boundaries; <code>iam:PermissionsBoundary</code> exists for exactly that purpose.</li>\n</ul>"
